GCCHECK(minor) reports DOOMED young survivors under BOCL_GCMINORSTRESS — a
remembered-set gap in the WIP generational minor collector (not any one caller).

Part of the generational GC work (see generational-gc.issue / the new-gc branch).
Filed from the v1.4.37 SORT work: the minor collector was used to stress-test the
new %KERNEL-SORT! primitive, and the probe fired — but on structures unrelated to
SORT (SORT's own scratch is never among the flagged survivors; see "Ruled out").

== Symptom ==
Under  BOCL_GCCHECK=1 BOCL_GCMINORSTRESS=1 ./bocl ...  the minor collector's
post-mark verifier (verify_no_dangling_minor in sources/memory.c) prints many
lines of the form:

  GCCHECK(minor): survivor 0x… (marked objcount=1021 size=8184) slot N
                  -> DOOMED YOUNG 0x… (objcount=3 size=32)

i.e. a SURVIVOR (an old or marked block) directly references a YOUNG block that
the minor mark did NOT reach — the invariant "every old->young edge is in the
remembered set" is violated, so that young child would be swept while still
reachable. Per the verifier's own comment this is "the primary correctness probe
for step 2/3" of the generational collector, so a hit is a real invariant break
in the minor path (a missing remembered-set entry / write barrier, or a remset
rebuild that drops a still-needed entry).

Observed on ~460 edges in one run; the dominant flagged survivor was a single
large object vector (objcount=1021, size=8184 — a VectorOfObject of 1021 Refs)
whose slots point to hundreds of unmarked young conses, plus ~15 stray cons
(objcount=3) survivors with one DOOMED young child each.

== Repro ==
  BOCL_GCCHECK=1 BOCL_GCMINORSTRESS=1 ./sources/bocl --batch -q --quit </dev/null

The warnings appear during BOOT (prelude load), independent of any user program —
no SORT or user code is needed. Caveat: GCMINORSTRESS=1 forces a full minor GC on
EVERY allocation, so booting the ~8500-line prelude under it is extremely slow and
often does not complete within a normal timeout; use a larger interval
(BOCL_GCMINORSTRESS=N for a minor GC every N allocs) to get further, at the cost of
less coverage. NB: the minor collector only runs under this knob — normal
operation uses the major garbage_collect, which is clean (BOCL_GCCHECK alone, no
MINORSTRESS: a 2000-element sort ran with zero verify_no_dangling warnings).

== Ruled out ==
- NOT the SORT scratch. %KERNEL-SORT! (sources/bocl.c) allocates two scratch
  simple-vectors of EXACTLY N slots; in the runs that produced these warnings N was
  1500 and 800, and no objcount=1500/800 vector appears among the flagged
  survivors. The 1021-slot survivor is some other structure (identity
  unconfirmed — it is NOT a hash-table bucket vector: those start at
  HT_INITIAL_BUCKETS=16 and double, so they are powers of two, never 1021).
- NOT a naive missing barrier on hash-table insert. hash_table.c already barriers
  its bucket stores: ht_remember_buckets() calls gc_write_barrier on the bucket
  VectorOfObject after every raw st[bi]=node store (lines ~350/384) and after
  resize (~210). So if a hash table is involved, the gap is subtler than "insert
  forgot to barrier".

== Hypothesis (needs confirmation) ==
A remembered-set LIFECYCLE gap rather than a single missing barrier: a write
barrier records a container, but gc_rebuild_remset() (run after each minor GC,
sources/memory.c ~973) keeps only entries whose block_has_young_child() is
currently true — so an old container that momentarily has no young child is
DROPPED, and a later mutation that re-introduces a young child must re-barrier it.
If any store into a large old object vector (or the object-vector case generally)
misses that re-barrier, or if the container is written via a path that bypasses
gc_write_barrier, its young children go untracked at the next minor GC. The
1021-slot object vector is the concrete instance to chase first: identify what it
is (instrument make_bucket_vector / Array_slots callers, or dump the class/first
few slots when verify_no_dangling_minor fires), then find the store path that
mutates it without a surviving remset entry.

Note: could also be a false positive of the probe itself (e.g. the young child IS
reachable via another root the minor mark should have followed but didn't). Either
way it must be resolved before the generational (minor) collector is enabled by
default.
